//ERR 20 3 20 6
//MSG="This function does not return a value in all control paths"

// 79e_a_break_makes_a_while_true_fall_through.cpl with the `brk` one loop
// further in. The level is what makes it escape: the `2` reaches past the `for`
// and out of the `while true`, so `f(0)` leaves the loop and would run off the
// end of the body. A bare `brk` here would leave only the `for` and the
// `while true` would still never fall through — that is
// 32_a_break_from_an_inner_loop_does_not_escape_a_while_true.cpl, which has to
// keep compiling.
//
// So the check cannot scan a loop's body for the keyword; it has to resolve each
// `brk` to the loop it actually targets, and this pair is what separates the two.
//
// The body ends in `ret 1`, which is the second thing this case pins down: a
// loop whose body always returns still has to be scanned for breaks. Deciding
// the loop is terminal because its body is terminal would skip the scan and let
// this through, and 35_a_levelled_continue_does_not_make_a_while_true_fall_through.cpl
// is the same shape with `cnt 2`, which must not be scanned as an exit.

fn f n
    while true
        for i 0..10
            if i > n
                brk 2
        ret 1

fn main
    out "{f(0)}"
